osx-gpu: radeonsi on a big-endian host, GFX8: index buffers byte-swapped for the fetch

An index buffer holds host-order indices on a big-endian host (part one).
GFX6-7 swap them as they fetch (VGT_DMA_INDEX_TYPE.SWAP_MODE, part three);
GFX8 fetches index buffers through L2 and ignores that field. Measured on
Polaris 12 (Firebrand's E9173, 2026-11-13) with rdn_gltest -L: 16- and
32-bit indices from client memory and from buffer objects drew nothing for
every SWAP_MODE 0-3, set through PKT3_INDEX_TYPE and again as the context
register, with REQ_PATH and RDREQ_POLICY varied as well; the same indices
stored little-endian drew right with any of them; 8-bit indices (native on
GFX8, nothing to swap) drew right. Display lists, which st/mesa replays as
indexed draws, and everything Mesa turns into an indexed draw drew nothing.

So on GFX8 the draw reads a byte-swapped copy of the indices it uses, made
the way GFX7's ubyte indices are already widened (si_draw_vbo): user
indices are swapped on the CPU as they are uploaded, and a buffer object's
by a compute shader (si_create_index_swap_compute_shader, internal, so the
big-endian memory lowering leaves its loads alone) into a new buffer that
keeps the indices at their offsets. Indirect draws take the range
si_get_draw_start_count reads, as the ubyte path does.

Gated on GFX_VERSION == GFX8 inside the per-generation draw code: the GFX6
and GFX7 instantiations compile as before. The rdn winsys serves GFX8 only
where the kext sets RDN_VRAM_INFO64_LE_IBS (Firebrand), so this path is
behind that flag as well.

The swapped copy of a buffer object is kept with the buffer (si_resource
be_index_swap), made once by one dispatch over the whole buffer and made
again only after the buffer is written: a CPU write map or buffer_subdata,
new storage, or a GPU write through si_barrier_after_internal_op (copies,
clears). Buffers radeonsi cannot watch -- persistently mapped, mapped for
writing right now, bound as an SSBO, image or streamout target, over 16 MB,
or rewritten more than 8 times -- are swapped per draw, just the range the
draw reads, into the stream uploader. The first version made
a new buffer per draw sized to the range's end: on glthread's upload
buffer that ran the card out of video memory (frb_glbench draws-c on the
E9173, 2026-11-13). The copy is used only by the context whose command
stream made it.

GFX9 takes the same copy. Measured on Vega 20 (the MI50 on Firebrand,
2026-11-15) with rdn_gltest -L 1/2/3: with VGT_INDEX_TYPE.SWAP_MODE set as
on GFX6-7 and host-order indices, display lists drew nothing; with the
swapped copy all three modes drew right. RDN_GFX9_INDEX_SWAP=hw selects the
fetch swap instead of the copy on GFX9, the switch that measurement used
(a later revision of the hardware or its microcode can be tried with it).

CPU writes are counted, not flagged (2026-11-15). glthread keeps the
client-memory indices of every glDrawElements in upload buffers that it
maps once, with PIPE_MAP_THREAD_SAFE and without PIPE_MAP_PERSISTENT, and
fills from the application thread for as long as they live. The first
version marked a buffer stale at the write map only, so the first draw
from an upload buffer swapped the whole buffer and every later draw read
that copy: indices written after it were zeros and the draws drew nothing.
Seen on the MI50 as vbo_subdata_repro (dhewm3-leopard/leopard/tests) right
until its first glReadPixels, which is the first time the driver thread
catches up with the application, and wrong from then on (56 of 84 runs,
297894 of 459732 cells), and with a glFinish after the first quad 63 of the
first frame's 64 quads missing; in Doom 3 (dhewm3) as GUI quads stretched
into large triangles and smeared text. Polaris has the same path; GFX6-7
swap in the fetch and never had it. Now si_resource counts its write
mappings (be_index_swap_maps) and steps be_index_swap_seq at every write
map and unmap, on whichever thread maps: a buffer with a write mapping is
never cached, and a copy is used only while the sequence is the one it was
made at (read before the mapping count, so a write the copy missed always
shows). That also closes the same race for the threaded context's
unsynchronized maps. Draws from a buffer that is mapped for writing, up to
16384 indices, swap on the CPU from the mapping, as user indices do,
instead of one compute dispatch per draw.

